1) Confirm Harmonic is working with a built-in world
bash
# List available example worlds (paths can vary by install)
gz sim -s --help | grep -i sdf

# Try a standard example world
gz sim -s shapes.sdf
Let it run for ~10–15 seconds:

If the GUI opens and doesn’t crash → core Gazebo Sim 8.11 is functional.

If it fails, note the exact error (missing render engine, plugin, etc.).

You can also try other built-in worlds if available:

bash
gz sim -s double_pendulum.sdf
gz sim -s baylands.sdf
2) Verify ROS 2 Jazzy ↔ Gazebo Harmonic bridge
With ROS 2 Jazzy sourced:

bash
# Terminal 1: start Gazebo
gz sim -s shapes.sdf

# Terminal 2: start a simple bridge (clock is always available)
ros2 run ros_gz_bridge parameter_bridge \
  /clock@rosgraph_msgs/msg/Clock[gz.msgs.Clock
Then:

bash
# Terminal 3
ros2 topic echo /clock
If you see a stream of Clock messages, your ROS 2 ↔ Gazebo integration is working at a basic level.

3) Test sensor-style workload (LiDAR / camera class)
Gazebo Harmonic 8.11 is commonly used for LiDAR and camera simulations in ROS 2 projects.

If you already have a robot package (e.g., TurtleBot3, a custom mobile robot, or a manipulator):

bash
# Example pattern (adapt to your package)
ros2 launch <your_robot_gazebo> <your_robot>.launch.py
Then check sensor topics:

bash
ros2 topic list | grep -E 'scan|image|points|depth'

# Example:
ros2 topic echo /scan        # for 2D LiDAR
ros2 topic echo /camera/image_raw
ros2 topic echo /lidar/points
If messages appear with real data (not just headers), your sensor pipeline is functional.

If you tell me which robot or tutorial you’re following, I can give exact commands for that setup.

4) Light stress test for “advanced” readiness
To see if your laptop can handle heavier use cases:

bash
# Run a more complex world if available
gz sim -s baylands.sdf
While it’s running:

bash
# Monitor CPU usage of gz sim
top -p $(pgrep -d, -f "gz sim")
Watch:

FPS in Gazebo (bottom-left in the GUI).

Whether simulation stays close to real-time.

Whether adding more models/sensors makes it unusably slow.

This is a practical indicator of whether your hardware is enough for advanced scenarios (multi-robot, many sensors, complex environments).

5) Optional: targeted “advanced workload” test script
If you want a single script that runs a short simulation and checks key behaviors, here’s a minimal one tuned for gz-sim 8.11 + ROS 2:

bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# test_gz_harmonic_advanced.sh

set -e

RED='\033[0;31m'
GREEN='\033[0;32m'
YELLOW='\033[1;33m'
NC='\033[0m'

pass() { echo -e "${GREEN}[PASS]${NC} $1"; }
warn() { echo -e "${YELLOW}[WARN]${NC} $1"; }
fail() { echo -e "${RED}[FAIL]${NC} $1"; }

section() { echo -e "\n=== $1 ==="; }

section "GAZEBO VERSION"
gz_ver=$(gz sim --version 2>&1 | head -n1)
if echo "$gz_ver" | grep -q "8.11"; then
  pass "Gazebo Sim version looks like Harmonic 8.11: $gz_ver"
else
  warn "Gazebo Sim version: $gz_ver (expected ~8.11 for Harmonic)"
fi

section "LAUNCH WORLD (shapes.sdf) FOR 8 SECONDS"
if timeout 8 gz sim -s shapes.sdf >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # timeout returns 124 if it timed out (i.e., process ran successfully)
  if [ $? -eq 124 ]; then
    pass "gz sim launched shapes.sdf and ran for 8s without crash"
  else
    fail "gz sim exited early or crashed"
  fi
else
  # timeout printed its own message; treat non-124 as failure
  fail "gz sim failed to launch shapes.sdf"
fi

section "ROS 2 + GAZEBO BRIDGE CHECK"
if ! command -v ros2 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  fail "ros2 not found in PATH"
  exit 1
fi

if ros2 run ros_gz_bridge --help >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  pass "ros_gz_bridge executable found"
else
  fail "ros_gz_bridge not found (install ros-*-ros-gz-bridge)"
fi

section "SENSOR TOPIC SANITY"
# This is generic; adapt if you have a specific robot
topic_count=$(ros2 topic list 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
if [ "$topic_count" -gt 0 ]; then
  pass "ros2 topic list works ($topic_count topics)"
else
  fail "ros2 topic list returned nothing"
fi

echo -e "\nAdvanced functional tests done."
Usage:

bash
chmod +x test_gz_harmonic_advanced.sh
./test_gz_harmonic_advanced.sh
